---
servings: 2
title: Sous Vide Ribeye
tags: CSO, Sous Vide, AI
source.author: AI Cooking Agent
course: Main dish
prep time: 5 min
cook time: 3 hours
description: A damn good sous vide ribeye steak, cooked in a Convection Steam Oven.
---

= Salt Brining =

> Dry brining is not mainly about drying the surface; it's about salt penetrating the muscle. 
> Over several hours, salt draws moisture out, dissolves, and is reabsorbed deeper into the meat, 
> seasoning it internally and slightly modifying muscle proteins for better juiciness and tenderness.

Place the @ribeye steak{2}(1.5-2" thick, bone-in or boneless) on a wire rack on a plate and sprinkle both sides with the @kosher salt{2%tsp}.

> Use approximately 3/4 teaspoon of kosher salt per pound of meat. For a 1.5 lb ribeye, this is about 1-1/8 tsp, so adjust accordingly.

Place the rack in the refrigerator uncovered overnight. This allows the salt to penetrate and season the meat.

= Sous Vide Cooking =

If using a #sous vide cooker{} with a water bath, preheat it to your target temperature. If using a #Convection Steam Oven (CSO){}, preheat it on Sous Vide mode.

> Medium-rare: 129°F (54°C), Medium: 136°F (58°C)

Place the brined ribeyes with @rosemary{1}(sprig, fresh), @garlic{1%clove}(minced),
and @butter{1/2%tbsp}(unsalted) per steak in a vacuum-sealed bag or a resealable plastic bag
using the water displacement method. Ensure the bag(s) is sealed properly.
Multiple steaks can be placed in the same bag if they fit comfortably without overlapping.

Cook the ribeyes in the preheated water bath or CSO for 2-3 hours.

= Searing =

Once the ribeyes are done cooking, remove them from the bag, discard the aromatics,
and thoroughly pat them dry with paper towels. Rest them for at least 3 minutes to air-dry while preparing the pan.

Using an #infrared thermometer{}, preheat a #heavy cast iron pan{} over high heat until it reaches 500°F-550°F (260°C-285°C).
Add a small amount of @high smoke point oil{1%tbsp} (avocado or grapeseed oil) to the pan and swirl it around.

> Oil reflects infrared poorly, so measure the pan temperature directly on the metal surface in a couple of spots before adding oil.
> Smoke is not a good indicator of pan temperature since cast iron can smoke at lower temperatures depending on residue.

Once the pan is hot, add the ribeyes and sear without moving them for 30-45 seconds per side, or until a deep brown crust forms.
Use tongs to hold the steak on its edges to sear the sides as well.
Searing the outside of the steak adds flavor and texture, but the interior is already cooked to your desired doneness from the sous vide process.
